---
Title: Tables And Alerts
---

# Tables and alerts

This page covers two block constructs that share a common visual purpose —
presenting information in a structured, scannable form. *Tables* arrange data in
rows and columns; *alerts* set a passage apart in a coloured callout box. Both go
beyond plain GitHub-Flavored Markdown, and both render to HTML for the web and to
LaTeX for print from a single source.

JMarkdown's tables are an extended dialect that adds **column spanning**, **row
spanning**, **per-column alignment and width**, and two **headerless** forms — all
on top of ordinary GFM table syntax, so existing tables keep working unchanged.
Alerts are callouts in Obsidian's style — `> [!NOTE]`, `> [!warning] A title`,
`> [!tip]- Folded` — covering every Obsidian type (GitHub's five alerts among
them) plus JMarkdown's own `suggestion`, with icons, custom titles, folding and
custom types.

Throughout, the live demos show the source on the left and its rendered result on
the right, so the examples can never drift from what the parser actually does.

# Tables

A JMarkdown table is a block of pipe-delimited lines. The familiar GFM form is a
**header row**, a **separator row**, and zero or more **body rows**:

:::markdown-demo
| Letter | Number |
|--------|-------:|
| A      |      1 |
| B      |     22 |
| C      |    333 |
:::

The cells of every row are separated by the pipe character `|`. A leading and a
trailing pipe are conventional and recommended; the separator row (the dashes)
both marks where the header ends and — as we will see — carries the alignment and
width information for each column.

Inline markdown is processed inside every cell, so emphasis, code spans, links,
and inline math all work:

:::markdown-demo
| Construct | Example         |
|-----------|-----------------|
| *Emphasis*  | /slanted/ text |
| `Code span` | `f(x)`         |
| Math        | $e^{i\pi}+1=0$ |
:::

## Alignment

Put a colon at one or both ends of a column's dashes in the separator row to set
that column's horizontal alignment:

| Separator | Alignment |
|-----------|-----------|
| `:---`    | left      |
| `:---:`   | centre    |
| `---:`    | right     |
| `---`     | default (browser left) |

:::markdown-demo
| Left   | Centre | Right |
|:-------|:------:|------:|
| a      | b      | c     |
| longer | longer | longer |
:::

Alignment is emitted as an inline `text-align` style on each affected cell, and on
the LaTeX path it selects the `l`, `c`, or `r` column specifier in the generated
`tabular`.

## Column widths

A column's width can be set directly in the separator row by embedding a
percentage between the dashes — `---30%---`. The width is applied as a CSS `width`
on every cell in that column (and on the LaTeX path it becomes a fixed-width
paragraph column, `p{0.30\textwidth}`):

:::markdown-demo
| Term | Definition                         |
|------|--------------60%-------------------|
| API  | A contract between two programs.   |
| CSS  | A language for styling documents.  |
:::

The percentage must be a whole number from 1 to 100. Width and alignment can be
combined in the same column — `:---30%---:` is a centred 30%-wide column.

## Column spanning

To make a cell span several columns, end it with **extra trailing pipes** — one
pipe per additional column. A cell that should occupy three columns is closed with
three pipes (`|||`): the cell itself, plus two for the columns it absorbs.

:::markdown-demo
| H1      | H2      | H3      |
|---------|---------|---------|
| This cell spans all three columns |||
| Two columns             || One |
:::

The colspan count is exactly the number of consecutive pipes that close the cell.
The remaining cells on that row fill the columns that are left.

## Row spanning

To make a cell span downward into the row below, place a caret `^` at the **end of
the cell in the lower row**, in the column you want merged upward. The lower cell's
text (everything before the `^`) is appended to the cell above, and the two are
rendered as a single cell with a `rowspan`.

Column spanning and row spanning compose. In the table below the top-left cell
spans **two columns and two rows** at once — two trailing pipes give it the
colspan, and the `^` on the row beneath gives it the rowspan:

:::markdown-demo
| This header spans two       || Header A |
| columns and two rows       ^|| Header B |
|-------------|-----------:|----------|
| Cell A      | Cell B     | Cell C   |
:::

For a `^` to merge a cell upward, the cell above it must have the **same colspan**
and start in the **same column**; otherwise the caret is treated as ordinary text.

## Headerless tables

Not every table wants a header row. JMarkdown recognises two headerless forms.

### Separator-first

If the first line of the block is a separator row, the table has **no header** but
still gets per-column alignment and width from that separator. The rows beneath are
all body rows:

:::markdown-demo
|:------|:-----:|------:|
| Left  | Centre | Right |
| a     | b      | c     |
:::

### Pure pipe-delimited

If you want neither a header nor a separator, simply write pipe-delimited lines.
Every line must begin and end with a pipe (apart from surrounding whitespace), and
the column count is taken from the first row:

:::markdown-demo
| Apples  | 12 |
| Bananas |  8 |
| Cherries | 30 |
:::

A pure pipe-delimited table has no separator row, so it cannot carry alignment or
width — for those, use the separator-first form. Colspan (`||`) and rowspan (`^`)
still work in both headerless forms.

## Escaping pipes

Because `|` separates cells, a literal pipe inside a cell must be escaped with a
backslash, `\|`:

:::markdown-demo
| Operator | Meaning          |
|----------|------------------|
| `a \| b` | bitwise or       |
| `a \|\| b` | logical or     |
:::

## Tables in LaTeX

On the LaTeX path each table is emitted as a `tabular` environment, ruled with
**booktabs** — `\toprule` at the top, `\midrule` under the header, `\bottomrule`
at the foot, the publication style rather than `\hline` (the package is pulled
in automatically). Alignment maps to the `l` / `c` / `r` column specifiers, a
percentage width becomes a fixed-width paragraph column (`p{0.30\textwidth}`),
column spanning uses `\multicolumn`, and row spanning uses `\multirow`. Because
of `\multirow`, any document that uses row spanning needs `\usepackage{multirow}`
in its preamble.

A plain `tabular` cannot break across pages, so a long table would overflow a
printed page. JMarkdown handles this automatically: a table longer than twenty
body rows is emitted as a `longtable` instead (which still rules with booktabs
and repeats its header on each page), so very long tables flow across pages with
no change to your source.

## Table syntax reference

| Feature           | Syntax                         | Notes |
|-------------------|--------------------------------|-------|
| Header table      | header / separator / body rows | standard GFM |
| Headerless (aligned) | separator row first         | alignment + width, no header |
| Headerless (plain)   | pipe-delimited lines only   | no alignment or width |
| Left align        | `:---`                         | in separator row |
| Centre align      | `:---:`                        | in separator row |
| Right align       | `---:`                         | in separator row |
| Column width      | `---NN%---`                    | 1–100, in separator row |
| Column span       | trailing `||` (one pipe / extra column) | any row |
| Row span          | `^` at end of cell in lower row | colspan + column must match |
| Literal pipe      | `\|`                           | inside a cell |

# Alerts

An *alert* — Obsidian calls it a *callout*, and it is also known as an
admonition — is a blockquote tagged with a type marker on its first line.
JMarkdown renders it the way Obsidian does: an accent-coloured left border, a
light tint of the accent behind the text, and a title row with the type's icon.
The marker is `[!type]` as the first line of a blockquote:

:::markdown-demo
> [!NOTE]
> Highlights information that users should take into
> account, even when skimming.
:::

The body of the alert is ordinary markdown, so it may span several lines and
contain emphasis, lists, links, maths, code, and so on. Everything after the
`[!type]` line becomes the box's content. The marker is matched without regard
to case: `[!NOTE]`, `[!Note]` and `[!note]` are the same.

## The built-in types

GitHub's specification defines five alerts. JMarkdown accepts all five, each
with its own colour and icon:

:::markdown-demo
> [!TIP]
> Optional information to help a user be more successful.
:::

:::markdown-demo
> [!IMPORTANT]
> Crucial information necessary for users to succeed.
:::

:::markdown-demo
> [!WARNING]
> Critical content demanding immediate attention due to
> potential risks.
:::

:::markdown-demo
> [!CAUTION]
> Negative potential consequences of an action.
:::

In Obsidian's scheme, which JMarkdown follows, `important` is another name for
`tip` and `caution` another name for `warning`: each pair shares a colour and
an icon, while the title is the name you wrote — "Important", "Caution".
Obsidian defines a dozen more types besides, several with aliases of their own;
all are listed in the reference table at the end of this page. `question` is one
of them, and suits the marginal commentary that arises when drafting:

:::markdown-demo
> [!QUESTION]
> Is this assumption still warranted given the new data?
:::

JMarkdown adds one type of its own, `suggestion`, with a lightbulb:

:::markdown-demo
> [!SUGGESTION]
> Consider splitting this section into two.
:::

A type JMarkdown does not know is drawn as a note, titled with its own name —
`[!aside]` becomes a note headed "Aside" — as Obsidian draws one.

## Titles, folding and nesting

An alert with no title of its own is headed by its type as written,
capitalised: `[!NOTE]` and `[!note]` give "Note", `[!CAUTION]` gives "Caution",
`[!tldr]` gives "Tldr" (an abstract). Text after the marker replaces that title,
and may contain markdown:

:::markdown-demo
> [!warning] Mind the *gap*
> The title row keeps the type's icon and colour.
:::

A `-` straight after the marker makes the alert foldable, starting closed; a `+`
makes it foldable, starting open. The title becomes a summary line with a chevron
that opens and closes it — no script involved:

:::markdown-demo
> [!tip]- Click to open
> Hidden until the title is clicked.
:::

Alerts nest: inside an alert, a line starting `> >` begins another.

:::markdown-demo
> [!note] The outer alert
> Some text.
>
> > [!example] An alert inside it
> > More text.
:::

## How an alert renders

An alert becomes a `<div>` carrying both Obsidian's names (`callout`,
`data-callout`) and GitHub's (`markdown-alert-<type>`), so a stylesheet written
for either applies. For `> [!NOTE]` the markup is:

```html
<div class="callout markdown-alert markdown-alert-note" data-callout="note">
  <p class="callout-title markdown-alert-title"><svg class="callout-icon">…</svg><span class="callout-title-inner">Note</span></p>
  <div class="callout-content">…the body…</div>
</div>
```

A foldable alert is a `<details>` element instead, its title a `<summary>`. An
alias renders as its type — `[!caution]` gives `data-callout="warning"`, with
the warning's icon and colour — under its own title, "Caution".

## Icons and styling

The icons are Font Awesome's, written into the page as inline SVG, so they show
with no icon font loaded and print as they look. The colours, border, tint and
spacing come from the standard stylesheet (`jmarkdown.css`), keyed on
`data-callout`. To restyle a type, target it in your own stylesheet —
`.callout[data-callout='note'] { --callout: teal; }` recolours the note's border,
icon, title and tint at once.

In print, each alert becomes a box drawn to the same design, with the same icon:
see [Print rendering](print-rendering.html).

## Custom types

New types — or a new title, icon or colour for a built-in one — are defined in
the project's `.jmarkdown/config.json` (or the global `~/.jmarkdown/config.json`)
under `Callouts`:

```json
{
	"Callouts": [
		{ "name": "recipe", "title": "Recipe", "icon": "utensils",
		  "color": "rebeccapurple", "aliases": ["cook"] },
		{ "name": "warning", "color": "#c2410c" }
	]
}
```

- `name` — the `[!name]` it answers to: a letter, then letters, digits, `-` or
  `_`. A built-in's name changes that type.
- `title` — the default title (otherwise the name, capitalised).
- `icon` — a Font Awesome Free icon by name: `lightbulb`, or with its family,
  `regular:circle` or `brands:github`.
- `color` — a CSS colour: hex, `rgb()`, `hsl()` or a colour name. A colour too
  pale to read on the page is darkened until it does.
- `aliases` — further `[!alias]` spellings for the type.

Every definition is checked. One that is malformed — a name that is not a name,
an icon that does not exist, a colour that is not a colour — is skipped with a
warning naming the entry and the reason, and never half-applied.

## Alert type reference

| Type          | Also written                  | Icon                  |
|---------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------|
| note          |                               | pencil                |
| abstract      | summary, tldr                 | clipboard             |
| info          |                               | circle with an *i*    |
| todo          |                               | circled check         |
| tip           | hint, important               | flame                 |
| success       | check, done                   | check                 |
| question      | help, faq                     | circled question mark |
| warning       | caution, attention            | warning triangle      |
| failure       | fail, missing                 | cross                 |
| danger        | error                         | lightning bolt        |
| bug           |                               | bug                   |
| example       |                               | numbered list         |
| quote         | cite                          | quotation mark        |
| compatibility |                               | checklist             |
| suggestion    |                               | lightbulb             |

The first fourteen are Obsidian's (`compatibility` is Clew's addition, used in
Obsidian's own help vault); `suggestion` is JMarkdown's.

